# The two upstream projects carry their own git history.
OpenJarvis/
VoiceJarvisSrc/

# Runtime output, not source.
logs/
backups/
*.log
daemon-debug.log

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/

# Third-party services installed beside the repositories, not source of ours.
services/*

# Except the pseudoconsole holder, which is ours and which herdr's server will
# not start without. Its venv stays ignored; the script does not.
!services/herdr-console/
services/herdr-console/*
!services/herdr-console/serve.py
# And the script that rebuilds the second-opinion transcriber, which is ours
# even though what it builds is not. The environment itself stays ignored.
!services/rebuild-whisper-fallback.ps1
.gstack/

# Skill copies that hallmark's installer wrote into the project (the same 107
# files three times over). Third-party, like services/, and they would
# otherwise be four fifths of any graph built over this folder.
.agents/
.claude/
agent/skills/
# graphify's graph is derived from the code, not source: `graphify update .`
# rebuilds it from the AST in seconds and with no API call, so committing 2.3 MB
# of JSON that changes on every refactor buys nothing.
graphify-out/

# Review artefacts: the video is large and also lives in
# a Videos/Screen Recordings folder. Stills and text stay in the repo.
review-artefacts/*.mp4

# Render scratch: segments, frames and beds that ffmpeg writes on the way to a
# reel. Twenty-six gigabytes of it, and every byte rebuilds from the footage on
# E: plus the scripts beside this file. The catalog is the exception -- grading
# 132 clips for light and subject was real work and does not come back free.
clients/*/build/*
!clients/*/build/catalog/
!clients/*/build/photos/

# The same rule as build/, applied to the client jobs that predate it. Every
# reel is assembled the same way -- segments, a joined cut, a lettered cut, a
# marked cut, QC stills, a generated bed, a synthesised voice -- and all of it
# rebuilds from the sources and the script beside it. Four and a half
# gigabytes of that had been committed.
#
# The finished cuts are NOT here on purpose: clients/<job>/<job>.mp4 and
# hot-dog-zone/variants are what was actually delivered, and they stay in the
# repository. Only the working parts are ignored, and the text that describes
# them -- shot lists, subtitles, ASS and SRT -- stays too.
clients/*/render/*.mp4
clients/*/render/*.wav
clients/*/render/*.mov
clients/*/story/*.mp4
clients/*/story/*.wav
clients/*/review/
clients/*/frames/
clients/*/frames-check/
clients/*/frames-now/
clients/*/voice/
clients/*/vo/
clients/*/vo-*/
clients/*/music/
clients/*/sfx/
clients/*/reference/

# A 220 MB build artefact of the desktop app. It is produced by the build, so
# it does not belong in the history of the source.
Jarvis-Windows-x64.zip

# The restaurant promo's supplied footage: 400MB of source that came from his
# Downloads and can be copied back from there. The compositions are tracked.
clients/nostimolimou/restaurant-promo/assets/source/
clients/nostimolimou/restaurant-promo/node_modules/
clients/nostimolimou/restaurant-promo/.hyperframes/
clients/nostimolimou/restaurant-promo/snapshots/

# Screenshots taken while working on the HUD graph. Evidence, not source.
hud-before.png
hud-after*.png
.playwright-mcp/

# A loop file is the same reel written three times so it keeps playing in VLC
# while he watches it. It is a viewing convenience, not a deliverable, and it
# rebuilds with one concat from the cut that IS committed.
clients/*/out/BEST-*-loop.mp4

# ── Secrets, for when this repo is public ──────────────────────────────────
# Nothing here has ever been committed: the keys live in ~/.config/jarvis,
# outside the repo, which is the right shape and the reason there was nothing
# to clean up. These rules exist so that stays true the first time somebody
# drops a .env next to the code, which is how it usually goes wrong.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
secrets.json
credentials.json
**/nim-keys.json
**/you-key.json
**/contabo.json
**/beszel.json
# A real config carries llm_api_key and sip_password. The example does not.
config.json
!config.example.json
!**/config.example.json
# Local setup state: which integrations this machine has turned on.
setup-state.json
